"A flower is where a plant makes its seeds.": "d734195eeea8",
"A plant has a problem that an animal does not have. To make seeds, it usually needs something from another plant of its own kind. But it is rooted to the ground and cannot go and get it. A flower is the answer to that problem.": "edb9106e391f",
"A plant has a problem that an animal does not have. To make seeds, it usually needs something from another plant of its own kind. But it is rooted to the ground and cannot go and get it. A flower is the answer to that problem. Look into the middle of a flower. Around the center there is a ring of thin stalks, each with a small bag on top. The bags are called anthers, and they make pollen, a fine yellow dust. Right in the center stands the stigma. It is often sticky, and below it, at the bottom of the flower, are the ovules. Each ovule can become a seed. A seed begins when pollen from one flower lands on the stigma of another. Something has to carry it there. About four out of five kinds of flowering plants use animals: bees, butterflies, moths, flies, beetles, birds, and bats. The animal comes for nectar, a sugary drink, and carries pollen away on its body without meaning to. This is why so many flowers are bright and sweet-smelling. The color and the scent are advertising, and the nectar is the pay. Some flowers even have lines on their petals that point the way to the nectar. Other plants use the wind. Grasses and oak trees are like this. Their flowers are small and dull, with no scent, because the wind does not need to be invited. But the wind does not aim, so these plants make enormous amounts of light pollen, and they catch it with stigmas shaped like feathers. When a grain of pollen lands on the right stigma, it grows a thin tube down to an ovule. Then the ovule becomes a seed, and the part of the flower around it, the ovary, grows into a fruit. A tomato, a peach, and a bean pod were all once the middle of a flower.": "90e16cf24ec6",
